      "text": "                                                    CAP Theorem\nThe Setup\n\nEric Brewer proposed the CAP theorem in 2000 as a conjecture. Gilbert and Lynch formally \nproved it in 2002. It states:\n\nIn a distributed system that experiences a network partition, you cannot simultaneously \nguarantee both Consistency and Availability. You must choose one.\n\nThe three properties:\n\nC — Consistency: Every read returns the most recent write or an error. All nodes see the \nsame data at the same time. After a write completes, any subsequent read from any node \nreflects that write.\n\nA — Availability: Every request receives a response (not necessarily the most recent data). \nThe system never returns an error due to unavailability — it always responds, even if the \nresponse might be stale.\n\nP — Partition Tolerance: The system continues operating even when some nodes cannot \ncommunicate with each other due to network failures.\n\nWhy P Is Not Optional\n\nA network partition means two or more nodes in your system can't communicate.This happens\nconstantly in real distributed systems:\n\n- A network switch fails\n- A datacenter loses connectivity\n- A Kubernetes pod loses network access\n- A cloud region has an outage\n- A network cable is physically cut\n\nThe question is not \"will partitions happen?\" but \"when they do, what does your system do?\"\n\nIf you choose to NOT tolerate partitions (P), your system must shut down entirely when \nany two nodes lose connectivity — because you can't guarantee correctness otherwise. \nFor any real production system, complete shutdown is unacceptable. So P is always required. \nPartition tolerance is mandatory.\n\nThis reduces the choice to: during a partition, do you sacrifice C or A?\n\nCP vs AP — What Actually Happens During a Partition\nCP System (Consistency + Partition Tolerance)\n\nDuring a partition, the system refuses to serve requests that might return stale or incorrect\ndata. It returns an error instead.\n\nAnalogy: An ATM that goes offline when it can't reach the bank. It won't let you withdraw \nmoney because it can't verify your balance. It sacrifices availability (you can't use it) to \nguarantee consistency (you'll never get wrong information).\n\nWhat this looks like:\n\nNormal operation: Client → Node A → reads data → returns result\nDuring partition: Node A can't reach Node B (which has recent writes)\n                  Node A returns: 503 Service Unavailable\n                  (better to say \"I don't know\" than to return stale data)\n\nReal examples:\n\nHBase: Strongly consistent. During a partition, it stops serving reads/writes to affected \nregions.\nZookeeper: Returns errors when it can't reach a quorum of nodes.\nRedis (in cluster mode with majority requirements): If a primary loses connectivity to replicas,\nit can go into a read-only or unavailable state.\nBanking systems: A bank cannot show you a wrong balance. During a network partition, it \nreturns an error rather than potentially incorrect data.\nAP System (Availability + Partition Tolerance)\n\nDuring a partition, the system keeps serving requests using whatever data it has available, \neven if that data might be stale. Different nodes might return different values for the same \nkey. Eventually, when the partition heals, nodes converge to consistent state.\n\nAnalogy: A GPS that still shows your last known location when it loses satellite signal. It's \nnot accurate, but it gives you something to work with. When signal returns, it updates to \nthe correct position.\n\nWhat this looks like:\n\nNormal operation: Client → Node A → reads data → returns result\nDuring partition: Node A can't reach Node B (which has recent writes)\n                  Node A returns its local (possibly stale) data\n                  Client gets a response, might not be the absolute latest\n                  When partition heals: Node A and Node B sync up (eventual consistency)\n\nReal examples:\n\nCassandra: Every node accepts reads and writes. During a partition, nodes serve their local \ndata. When partition heals, they reconcile using timestamps.\nDynamoDB: Always available. During partition, returns best available data.\nCouchDB: AP by design. Conflicts are resolved at the application level.\nDNS: When DNS servers are partitioned, you might get cached (stale) responses. This is \nacceptable — slightly stale DNS is better than no DNS.\n\n                                    The Nuance Beyond the Binary\n\nCAP is widely misunderstood because it presents a binary choice that doesn't capture reality:\n\n1. Partitions are rare, normal operation matters more.\nCAP describes behavior during partitions. Most of the time, there's no partition. Whatmatters \nin normal operation is the consistency model you provide. A Cassandra cluster (AP) can be \nconfigured to behave nearly like a CP system in normal operation using quorum settings — it's \nonly during actual partitions that the AP choice kicks in.\n\n2. Consistency is a spectrum, not a binary.\n\"Consistent\" can mean many different things (we'll cover this in the next section). CAP's \"C\" \nspecifically means linearizability — the strongest consistency model. Many systems offer \nweaker consistency that still satisfies most application requirements without the availability \nsacrifice.\n\n3. You can tune per-operation.\nCassandra lets you choose consistency per query:\n\nCONSISTENCY ONE     → fastest, weakest consistency (1 node must respond)\nCONSISTENCY QUORUM  → balanced (majority of nodes must respond)\nCONSISTENCY ALL     → strongest, slowest (every node must respond)\n\nThis isn't \"picking two of three\" — it's choosing your position on the consistency-availability \ntradeoff per operation.\n\n4. The theorem applies to a specific conflict — not all operations.\nRead-only operations don't create consistency conflicts. The CAP tradeoff only matters for \nread-after-write scenarios where different nodes might have different state.\n\nINTERVIEW TIP\nNever just say \"CAP theorem says you can only have two of three.\" Show you understand \nthat P is mandatory, the real choice is C vs A during partitions, and that modern systems \ntune this per-operation. Then discuss what \"consistency\" actually means — which leads \ndirectly into the next section.\nCAP Theorem, the real choice is C vs A during partitions, and that modern systems tune this\nper-operation. Then discuss what \"consistency\" actually means — which leads directly into \nthe next section - Strong vs Eventual Consistency.",
