      "text": "                                                Quick Additional Details/Concepts/Notes\n\n                                            The PACELC Theorem (The Sequel to CAP)\n\nCAP is great, but it only tells me what happens when things break (during a network partition). What about the\nother 99% of the time when the network is perfectly fine? Interviewers love this follow-up, which is where PACELC\ncomes in.\n\nIt stands for: If Partition (P), trade Availability (A) vs. Consistency (C); Else (E), trade Latency (L) vs. \nConsistency (C).\n\nThe \"Else\" Scenario: When my network is totally healthy, I still have to make a core architectural tradeoff.\n\nTrading L for C (Latency over Consistency): If I want my system to be perfectly consistent (like a strict CP \ndatabase), I have to accept higher Latency because the nodes must take the time to synchronize and lock before \nreturning a response.\n\nTrading C for L (Consistency over Latency): If I want my system to be blazing fast (low Latency, like an AP \ndatabase by default), I have to accept weaker Consistency, knowing that I am returning data instantly without \nwaiting for all nodes to sync up.\n\n                                    CAP Applies to Everything (Not Just Databases)\n\nIt is an easy trap to limit CAP theorem entirely to database selection. CAP actually applies to any distributed \nsystem, including my own application architecture and microservices.\n\nIf my Node.js API orchestrates a checkout flow and needs to synchronously call a Payment microservice and a \nShipping microservice, what happens if the Shipping service goes offline (a Partition)? I have to make a CAP choice \ndirectly in my business logic:\n\nThe CP Choice: I fail the entire checkout request and return a 503 to the client. I sacrifice availability to ensure \nthe system's state remains perfectly consistent (no payment without shipping confirmation).\n\nThe AP Choice: I process the payment, return a 200 OK to the user, and drop a message in a queue to handle the \nshipping later. I keep the system highly available, but I've temporarily sacrificed consistency because the user thinks \nthe order is fully complete when the shipping backend hasn't actually processed it yet.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                Quick Additional Details/Concepts/Notes\n\n                                            The PACELC Theorem (The Sequel to CAP)\n\nCAP is great, but it only tells me what happens when things break (during a network partition). What about the\nother 99% of the time when the network is perfectly fine? Interviewers love this follow-up, which is where PACELC\ncomes in.\n\nIt stands for: If Partition (P), trade Availability (A) vs. Consistency (C); Else (E), trade Latency (L) vs. \nConsistency (C).\n\nThe \"Else\" Scenario: When my network is totally healthy, I still have to make a core architectural tradeoff.\n\nTrading L for C (Latency over Consistency): If I want my system to be perfectly consistent (like a strict CP \ndatabase), I have to accept higher Latency because the nodes must take the time to synchronize and lock before \nreturning a response.\n\nTrading C for L (Consistency over Latency): If I want my system to be blazing fast (low Latency, like an AP \ndatabase by default), I have to accept weaker Consistency, knowing that I am returning data instantly without \nwaiting for all nodes to sync up.\n\n                                    CAP Applies to Everything (Not Just Databases)\n\nIt is an easy trap to limit CAP theorem entirely to database selection. CAP actually applies to any distributed \nsystem, including my own application architecture and microservices.\n\nIf my Node.js API orchestrates a checkout flow and needs to synchronously call a Payment microservice and a \nShipping microservice, what happens if the Shipping service goes offline (a Partition)? I have to make a CAP choice \ndirectly in my business logic:\n\nThe CP Choice: I fail the entire checkout request and return a 503 to the client. I sacrifice availability to ensure \nthe system's state remains perfectly consistent (no payment without shipping confirmation).\n\nThe AP Choice: I process the payment, return a 200 OK to the user, and drop a message in a queue to handle the \nshipping later. I keep the system highly available, but I've temporarily sacrificed consistency because the user thinks \nthe order is fully complete when the shipping backend hasn't actually processed it yet.",
