      "updated": 1790418177252,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The CP Choice (Consistency over Availability)\nIn this approach, my controller demands 100% synchronization. If the ShippingService is unreachable, I actively abort the operation, roll back any previous steps, and return an \nerror to the client. The system remains perfectly consistent, but it is unavailable.\n\nTypeScript\nasync function processCheckoutCP(orderData: Order) {\n  try {\n    // We attempt to process both synchronously\n    const payment = await PaymentService.charge(orderData);\n    const shipping = await ShippingService.createLabel(orderData);\n\n    // If both succeed, the system is consistent and we return success\n    return { \n        status: 200, \n        message: \"Order complete!\", \n        data: { payment, shipping } \n    };\n\n  } catch (error) {\n    // THE CP CHOICE: If Shipping throws a network error (a partition), \n    // we refuse to leave the system in a half-finished state.\n    \n    // 1. Compensating transaction to undo the payment\n    await PaymentService.refund(orderData);\n    \n    // 2. Fail the entire request (Sacrificing Availability)\n    throw new HttpException(\n        \"503 Service Unavailable: Could not complete order at this time.\", \n        503\n    );\n  }\n}\nThe AP Choice (Availability over Consistency)\nIn this approach, my controller prioritizes a successful user experience. If the ShippingService is unreachable, I catch the error, offload the work to an asynchronous message \nqueue, and tell the user the order was successful. The system is highly available, but temporarily inconsistent (the order exists without a shipping label).\n\nTypeScript\nasync function processCheckoutAP(orderData: Order) {\n  // 1. Process payment synchronously (assuming payment must succeed)\n  const payment = await PaymentService.charge(orderData);\n\n  try {\n    // 2. Try to hit the Shipping service synchronously\n    const shipping = await ShippingService.createLabel(orderData);\n    \n    return { \n        status: 200, \n        message: \"Order complete!\", \n        data: { payment, shipping } \n    };\n\n  } catch (error) {\n    // THE AP CHOICE: Shipping is down (a partition), but we DO NOT fail the request.\n    \n    // 1. Publish the task to a message queue (like RabbitMQ or SQS) to be processed \n    // asynchronously once the Shipping service comes back online.\n    await MessageQueue.publish('shipping-retry-queue', { \n        orderId: orderData.id, \n        paymentId: payment.id \n    });\n    \n    // 2. Return success to the user (Sacrificing immediate Consistency for Availability)\n    return { \n      status: 200, \n      message: \"Order placed successfully! Your shipping confirmation will be emailed shortly.\", \n      data: { payment, shipping: \"pending\" } \n    };\n  }\n}",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The CP Choice (Consistency over Availability)\nIn this approach, my controller demands 100% synchronization. If the ShippingService is unreachable, I actively abort the operation, roll back any previous steps, and return an \nerror to the client. The system remains perfectly consistent, but it is unavailable.\n\nTypeScript\nasync function processCheckoutCP(orderData: Order) {\n  try {\n    // We attempt to process both synchronously\n    const payment = await PaymentService.charge(orderData);\n    const shipping = await ShippingService.createLabel(orderData);\n\n    // If both succeed, the system is consistent and we return success\n    return { \n        status: 200, \n        message: \"Order complete!\", \n        data: { payment, shipping } \n    };\n\n  } catch (error) {\n    // THE CP CHOICE: If Shipping throws a network error (a partition), \n    // we refuse to leave the system in a half-finished state.\n    \n    // 1. Compensating transaction to undo the payment\n    await PaymentService.refund(orderData);\n    \n    // 2. Fail the entire request (Sacrificing Availability)\n    throw new HttpException(\n        \"503 Service Unavailable: Could not complete order at this time.\", \n        503\n    );\n  }\n}\nThe AP Choice (Availability over Consistency)\nIn this approach, my controller prioritizes a successful user experience. If the ShippingService is unreachable, I catch the error, offload the work to an asynchronous message \nqueue, and tell the user the order was successful. The system is highly available, but temporarily inconsistent (the order exists without a shipping label).\n\nTypeScript\nasync function processCheckoutAP(orderData: Order) {\n  // 1. Process payment synchronously (assuming payment must succeed)\n  const payment = await PaymentService.charge(orderData);\n\n  try {\n    // 2. Try to hit the Shipping service synchronously\n    const shipping = await ShippingService.createLabel(orderData);\n    \n    return { \n        status: 200, \n        message: \"Order complete!\", \n        data: { payment, shipping } \n    };\n\n  } catch (error) {\n    // THE AP CHOICE: Shipping is down (a partition), but we DO NOT fail the request.\n    \n    // 1. Publish the task to a message queue (like RabbitMQ or SQS) to be processed \n    // asynchronously once the Shipping service comes back online.\n    await MessageQueue.publish('shipping-retry-queue', { \n        orderId: orderData.id, \n        paymentId: payment.id \n    });\n    \n    // 2. Return success to the user (Sacrificing immediate Consistency for Availability)\n    return { \n      status: 200, \n      message: \"Order placed successfully! Your shipping confirmation will be emailed shortly.\", \n      data: { payment, shipping: \"pending\" } \n    };\n  }\n}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
